## Built for one

Seven square metres, and we would rather say that plainly than dress it up. This is the smallest room in the house: a single bed with a boxspring mattress, a desk, a flat-screen television and a bathroom of your own, and not a great deal of floor between them. If you are travelling alone and mean to spend your days out in the city, it is the sensible way to sleep a few streets from the Albert Cuypmarkt without paying for space you will not use.

What it lacks in size it does not lack in finish. The bed sits against the same panel of sage geometric wallpaper the rest of the hotel uses, with a black reading lamp on the wall beside it and the deep red patterned carpet running underneath. A tall window looks straight out over Ceintuurbaan and the plane trees along it, which does more for the room than another two square metres would. The desk is by the window, and there is a radiator beneath it.

The bathroom is white-tiled with a corner shower, a wall-hung basin and a heated rail.

Reception is downstairs around the clock, so a late arrival is no trouble, and there is luggage storage if your train leaves long after check-out. Breakfast runs from 07:30 to 10:30 at the front of the hotel.

Free WiFi throughout. One guest only: the room genuinely does not take two.
